Recursion is a programming technique where a function calls itself to solve a problem by breaking it down into smaller, 
identical subproblems. Instead of using loops (for or while), the function repeats its logic by calling itself with
modified inputs until it reaches a stopping condition.

# Basic Syntax in Python :-
Every recursive function in Python relies on two essential parts:

Code :- 

def recursive_function(parameter):
  # 1. Base Case: The condition that stops the recursion
  if base_condition:
    return base_value

  # 2. Recursive Case: The function calls itself with a smaller/modified input
  else:
    return recursive_function(modified_parameter)

Base Case :- The stopping condition. Without it, the function will call itself forever until Python crashes with a RecursionError.
Recursive Case :- The part where the function calls itself, moving the input closer to the base case with each step.


Key Tips to Remember

• Always include a return statement before your recursive call if you expect a calculated result back; otherwise, Python will return None.
• Watch the recursion limit. Python limits recursion depth to 1,000 calls by default to prevent infinite loops from crashing your computer's 
memory (you can check this using sys.getrecursionlimit()). For massive datasets, standard loops are usually faster and safer.